########################################################################################################################
# Help for the PowerShell port (preview)
########################################################################################################################
Nixlper also exists as a PowerShell module (Windows PowerShell 5.1 / PowerShell 7), for when you have to work in
PowerShell but think in bash. Each GitHub release ships nixlper-powershell-vX.Y.Z.zip: unblock it, extract it
into your modules folder, then load it from your PowerShell $PROFILE:

  Unblock-File nixlper-powershell-vX.Y.Z.zip
  Expand-Archive nixlper-powershell-vX.Y.Z.zip -DestinationPath ($env:PSModulePath -split [IO.Path]::PathSeparator)[0]
  Import-Module Nixlper            # or, from a clone: Import-Module <clone>\src\main\powershell\Nixlper\Nixlper.psd1

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Same shortcuts as bash (PSReadLine chords: CTRL+X, then the second key)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
[CTRL + X then A] or  fa : command palette - search and run any nixlper command
[CTRL + X then D] or  bd : display bookmarks and jump to one (fzf when installed)
[CTRL + X then B] or  bm : add or remove a bookmark for the current folder

The bookmarks file uses the same format as bash and can be shared between the two shells.
Typing a bookmark's name jumps to it.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Bash-style commands (Windows by default)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  grep   -i -v -n -r -l -c -w -x -o -F -E -e --include      (GNU basic-regex rules: 'a\|b', [[:digit:]])
  head   -n N | -N
  tail   -n N | -N | -n +N | -f
  wc     -l -w -c                                          (ls | wc -l counts items)
  ls     -a -l -R -t -S -r -1 -d
  rm     -r -f -i -v
  cp     -r -f -i -v
  mv     -f -i -v
  touch  [-c] FILE...
  which  [-a] COMMAND...
  export NAME=VALUE | -n NAME

PowerShell-style calls (ls -Recurse, rm -Force, Get-ChildItem *.tmp | rm) still work: they are passed to the
original PowerShell commands.

Differences: PowerShell swallows a bare "--" (use grep -e -PATTERN); environment variables are $env:NAME.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Configuration (environment variables, set before Import-Module):

  NIXLPER_BASH_COMPAT       auto|true|false  auto  bash-style commands (auto = Windows only, real executables win)
  NIXLPER_BOOKMARKS_FILE    path                   Bookmarks file (default: ~/.local/share/nixlper/bookmarks)
  NIXLPER_BOOKMARKS_FUZZY   bool             true  Use fzf for the bookmark picker when installed
  NIXLPER_PS_KEYBINDINGS    bool             true  Bind the CTRL+X chords (on Windows, CTRL+X then no longer cuts)

Full documentation: https://minhounet.github.io/nixlper/feature-powershell.html
